				<h1>Open-source Conductor alternative: Zoes vs Conductor</h1>
				<div class="post-meta">By Minh Ngo · Published <time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time> · Updated <time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time></div>
				<p class="lede">Zoes is a free, MIT-licensed alternative to Conductor for running coding agents in parallel git worktrees with diff review. It supports ten agent CLIs, keeps local sessions running after you close the window, publishes Linux and Windows builds, and includes automations, a ticket Board and a CLI, with no paid tier.</p>
				<div class="callout">
					<p><span class="callout-title">Disclosure</span>The Zoes team wrote this. Conductor facts come from <a href="https://www.conductor.build/">conductor.build</a> and its docs, checked on 18 November 2026. Both products ship often, so check the dates. If something here is out of date, <a href="https://github.com/mingo023/anywhere/issues">open an issue</a>.</p>
				</div>

				<h2 id="conductor">Conductor in one paragraph</h2>
				<p>Conductor's own line is "Run parallel coding agents on your Mac." Its homepage goes on: "Create parallel Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor agents in isolated workspaces. See at a glance what they're working on, then review and merge their changes." Its docs put the split this way: "A harness is the agent runtime that writes code; Conductor is the workspace layer around it." It supports four harnesses, Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, and OpenCode, and ships Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode bundled, so there is less to install. Each workspace has its own branch. Conductor says it is free to use and doesn't bill or resell model usage. Conductor Cloud runs workspaces in a sandbox under Pro, Teams, and Enterprise plans, and an iOS app shipped in version 0.90.0 on 2 October 2026. Review and merge is the core of the product, and it shows.</p>

				<h2 id="anywhere">Zoes in one paragraph</h2>
				<p>Zoes is a desktop app for watching and driving many coding agents at once. The window is Rust on GPUI. A Go background service, pocketd, owns the terminals and sessions. It runs the agent CLIs you already installed and signed in to, puts each session under a project and a git worktree, tells you which one needs you, and keeps the diff and the pull request beside the session. The whole thing, desktop app, daemon, iPhone app, and push relay, is in one MIT-licensed repository.</p>

