				<h2 id="where-anywhere-is-different">Where Zoes is different</h2>

				<h3>Open source, MIT, no paid tier</h3>
				<p>Zoes's site says it plainly: "There is no account to make, no seat to buy and no feature held back for a paid plan. There never will be." The pricing table lists the desktop app, the daemon and CLI, and the push relay at $0, and the cloud tier and premium tier as "Never". The architecture page adds that there is no license check in the code. You can read every line, build it yourself, and fork it.</p>

				<h3>Ten agent CLIs, including Grok Build, Pi, omp, fx, Hermes Agent, and Antigravity</h3>
				<p>Zoes doesn't bundle agents. It runs the CLIs it finds on your login <code>PATH</code>, signed in to your own accounts, and never proxies a model or resells a token. That is one more install step than a bundle, and in return you choose the version, and you can use six agents beyond the four Conductor supports. Claude Code and Codex run in a real terminal or as a chat and report their status from both. The other eight run as a chat. The commands and sign-in steps are in <a href="/docs/agents">Supported agents</a>.</p>

				<h3>Local sessions that outlive the window</h3>
				<p>Conductor's Cloud FAQ is clear about its local workspaces: "Local workspace sessions run on your Mac. If you close the app or shut down your Mac, those sessions end." Its answer for long runs is Cloud. Zoes's answer is a local daemon. pocketd runs every session and starts at login, so closing the window doesn't stop an agent, and on macOS the app itself stays in the background by default. See <a href="/docs/sessions">Sessions and status</a> and <a href="/blog/coding-agents-keep-running-after-closing-window">Keep coding agents running after you close the window</a>.</p>

				<h3>Linux and Windows builds, and SSH hosts</h3>
				<p>Zoes publishes Linux and Windows archives next to the Mac app. To be straight about them: they are unsigned and have no updater, and you run <code>pocket-desktop</code> from the unpacked folder. You can also add a Mac, Linux, or Windows machine as a host over SSH. Zoes installs pocketd there, and that host's projects show in your sidebar while their agents run on the host. See <a href="/docs/hosts">Run agents on another machine over SSH</a>.</p>

				<h3>Automations, Board, and the <code>zoes</code> CLI are included</h3>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="/docs/automations">Automations</a> start a prompt on chosen weekdays at a time, or every few minutes, and keep firing while the desktop app is closed.</li>
					<li>The <a href="/docs/board">Board</a> holds tickets across projects. A started ticket gets its own worktree and session.</li>
					<li>The <a href="/docs/cli"><code>zoes</code> command</a> starts sessions, sends prompts, waits for results, and edits tickets from any shell.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2 id="when-conductor-may-fit-better">When Conductor may fit better</h2>
				<div class="callout">
					<p><span class="callout-title">Conductor is the better pick if you want</span></p>
					<ul>
						<li>Claude Code, Codex, and OpenCode installed for you, as a bundle, instead of installing each CLI yourself.</li>
						<li>A long-shipped, tightly scoped review and merge workflow on macOS, including workspaces from pull requests and stacks.</li>
						<li>Cloud workspaces in a managed sandbox, the Conductor API, and shared workspaces.</li>
						<li>An iOS app you can install today.</li>
						<li>Team and Enterprise features such as multiplayer and an admin portal. Zoes doesn't offer these and has said it won't sell a cloud tier.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>

				<h2 id="moving-a-workflow-over">Moving a workflow over</h2>
				<p>The model is the same: one isolated checkout and branch per task, an agent in it, review, merge. One Conductor workspace per task maps to one Zoes worktree per task.</p>
				<ol>
					<li>Install and sign in to the CLIs you used through Conductor, for example <code>claude auth login</code>, <code>codex login</code>, <code>opencode auth login</code>, or <code>cursor-agent login</code>.</li>
					<li>Install Zoes and add your repository as a project.</li>
					<li>Move workspace setup into <code>.zoes/config.json</code> or <code>.zoes/setup.sh</code>, so new worktrees install dependencies before the agent starts.</li>
					<li>Press <kbd>⌘⇧N</kbd> to start a session in a new worktree, from a branch or from a pull request number.</li>
					<li>Review in <strong>Changes</strong> and merge from the pull request card.</li>
				</ol>
				<p>The step-by-step version is <a href="/blog/run-multiple-claude-code-agents-in-parallel">How to run multiple Claude Code agents in parallel</a>. For the wider field, see <a href="/blog/best-tools-run-multiple-coding-agents">the best tools to run multiple coding agents in parallel</a>.</p>

				<section class="faq" aria-labelledby="faq">
					<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
					<h3>Is there an open-source alternative to Conductor?</h3>
					<p>Zoes is a free, MIT-licensed alternative to Conductor for running coding agents in parallel git worktrees with diff review. It supports ten agent CLIs, keeps local sessions running after you close the window, publishes Linux and Windows builds, and includes automations, a ticket Board and a CLI, with no paid tier.</p>
					<h3>Does Zoes work on Linux or Windows?</h3>
					<p>Yes. Zoes publishes builds for Linux on x86_64 and Windows on x64. They are unsigned and have no updater, and the binary is pocket-desktop. The macOS app needs macOS 13 or later and is universal, signed and notarized. Conductor's desktop app is macOS only.</p>
					<h3>Which agents does Zoes support compared with Conductor?</h3>
					<p>Zoes runs ten agent CLIs that you install and sign in to yourself: Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, Grok Build, OpenCode, Pi, omp, fx, Hermes Agent and Antigravity. Conductor supports Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and OpenCode, and bundles Claude Code, Codex and OpenCode.</p>
					<h3>Do agents keep running if I close the app?</h3>
					<p>In Zoes, yes. pocketd owns the terminals and sessions, so agents keep running after the window closes, and scheduled automations keep firing. Conductor's docs say local workspace sessions end if you close the app or shut down the Mac, and Conductor Cloud workspaces keep working instead.</p>
					<h3>Is Zoes free?</h3>
					<p>Yes. Zoes is MIT licensed and costs $0, with no account, no cloud tier and no premium tier. It runs the agent CLIs you already use and never proxies a model or resells a token.</p>
